      "label":"World-model result domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Index for urn:stdo:bounded-context:graph-native-odd, declared by ODD §1: ODD/Outcome-Driven Development and GTL/Graph Transformation Language are owned at ODD #1-position; ABG has that owner and relation #abg; Edge Traversal/Traversal is owned at #traversal; Probabilistic Compute, F_D, F_P and F_H at #probabilistic-compute, with F_P and F_H additionally related through SPEC_METHOD #5-ambiguity-governance-and-lifecycle. Closure decisions indexed at ODD #115d-constructive-evaluation-and-yield-loop are close, yield, retry(defeated), retry(undecided), repair(implementation), repair(proof), re-enter(undeclared), re-enter(basis), reprice, block(undeclared), block(basis). Audit outcomes indexed at ODD #9-method-governance-and-audit are compliant, non-compliant, valid and worth incorporating. Both result rows refer to FOUNDATIONS #results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":313,
          "quote":"Context declared by [ODD §1](ODD.md#1-position) (P3). `F_D`, `F_P` and\n`F_H` are also indexed in the axiomatic-calculus context in §4; the two\nrows create no identity between the contexts (P1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F14.%20Graph-native%20ODD",
          "start_line":298
        }
      ],
      "key":"graph_native_odd_index",
      "label":"Graph-native ODD index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Index for urn:stdo:bounded-context:traversal-occurrence-profile, declared by TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE #position. Traversal Occurrence Profile/Sigma_occurrence is owned there and relates through #imported-calculus-boundary. Other indexed identities and owners are Occurrence/#occurrence-identity, Framework Event/#candidate-judgment-event-and-relation-separation, Typed Lineage/#causality-and-typed-lineage, Mutable Subject/#mutable-subject-effect-relation, Event Frontier/#event-frontier-and-projection, and Traversal Occurrence Profile Basis/#exact-profile-identity. Cause-separated stop and decision values are refusal(constraint), refusal(basis), refused(constraint), refused(basis), partial_effect(not_attained), partial_effect(undecided), partial(not_attained), partial(undecided), owned at #closed-constraint-judgment-residual-and-stop-kinds and related to AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS AC-007 and FOUNDATIONS #results. The index supplies neither their operational rules nor an equivalence between differently spelled values.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":329,
          "quote":"| stop and decision values split by cause: `refusal(constraint)`; `refusal(basis)`; `refused(constraint)`; `refused(basis)`; `partial_effect(not_attained)`; `partial_effect(undecided)`; `partial(not_attained)`; `partial(undecided)` | `urn:stdo:bounded-context:traversal-occurrence-profile` | [`TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE.md#closed-constraint-judgment-residual-and-stop-kinds`](TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE.md#closed-constraint-judgment-residual-and-stop-kinds) | [`AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#ac-007-constraint-and-refusal`](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#ac-007-constraint-and-refusal); [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F15.%20Traversal%20occurrence",
          "start_line":329
        }
      ],
      "key":"traversal_occurrence_index",
      "label":"Traversal-occurrence index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Member-owned concepts outside every bounded context declared by their owner. FOUNDATIONS owns: recovered, independent, derived, hypothesis (#1-vocabulary-of-standing); algorithmic description, informational interpretation, computational realisation (#accounts-of-a-construction); Import, Import Declaration, Adoption, Adoption Premise (#imports-between-members); Material Family, Family Table, Instance Premise, Added Choice, Adopting Product (#foundation-to-law); Supporting Evidence of a categorical claim, superseding Evidence Class (#4-algebra, Formal fragment); Join in four senses—categorical join, composition boundary, join of results, relation join (#1-vocabulary-of-standing); Assurance Relations, Assertion Provenance, Execution Provenance, Observation Sufficiency, Impact Cover, Validity Dependency, Mandatory Gate (#assurance-relations). ACTIVATION owns: Current State, Destination, Transition (#transitions); Construction Relation, Strategy, Composition Boundary, Feedback and Diagnosis, the four causes (#construction-relation); Configuration (#configuration); Acquisition, Derived Surface, deciding owner section (#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope); Rewrite Step, Licensing Law, Exact Step, Approximate Step, Adequacy Relation, End-to-End Bound (#refinement); Truth Surface and actor's account (#the-workspace-and-an-actors-account). EVALUATION owns Equivalence Ladder, semantic equivalence of realisations, Computational Equivalence, Categorical Equivalence (#6-conformance-of-realisations), and Adequate Attention, Actual-Use Evidence (#frame-qualification). CONFORMANCE owns Discriminator and Common Discriminators (#conformance), and Logical-Review Check (#5-coverage-of-the-common-discriminators). PROFILES owns the profile import rule (#1-the-boundary). Semantic equivalence of realisations is explicitly distinct from Semantic Equivalence of concepts in glossary §5.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":340,
          "quote":"This section indexes the concepts that a member defines outside every\nbounded context it declares. The source context of each is its owning\nmember",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":333
        }
      ],
      "key":"member_owned_vocabulary_index",
      "label":"Member-owned vocabulary without a declared context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Member-context result indexes: DESIGN_MODULE axiom verdict {pass, fail, not_applicable} at #cross-view-axiom-evaluation; Ontology verdict and design verdict each index accepted at #gate-and-retrospective-work, related to ACTIVATION #disposition. UX #15-results-of-this-profile owns surface review {compliant, violation, design_module_incomplete}, replay proof {replay_passes, replay_fails}, closure obligations {closure_obligations_met, closure_unproven}, carrier admission {carrier_admitted, carrier_missing}. All except the acceptance row relate to FOUNDATIONS #results. These lists retain their distinct predicates and do not establish any result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":368,
          "quote":"| axiom verdict: `pass`; `fail`; `not_applicable` | DESIGN_MODULE | [`DESIGN_MODULE.md#cross-view-axiom-evaluation`](DESIGN_MODULE.md#cross-view-axiom-evaluation) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":363
        }
      ],
      "key":"member_owned_result_indexes",
      "label":"Member-owned result indexes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Occurrence-specific sense index. indeterminate: core result where admissible evidence cannot decide (FOUNDATIONS #results), or triage payload class, not result (EVALUATION #result-and-triage-projection). not_applicable: triage payload class, not result (same EVALUATION clause); successor disposition with bounded reason (RELEASE §9); lifecycle phase declared not to apply with reason (SPEC_METHOD #operational-lifecycle-sufficiency); design-grounded exclusion and entity-lifecycle matrix entry (DESIGN_MODULE head/state model and ACTIVATION §4); traversal judgment value (TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE stop-kinds clause). refusal/refused: method-function activation or translation refusal (REFERENCE_FRAME §5); a_c constraint or basis refusal (AC-007); traversal judgment decision and stop (TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE); work-contract admission refusal (ACTIVATION #admission). gap: a_c stop with a core value (AC-007), or conjunction gap with no own value (FOUNDATIONS #conjunction-of-results). disposition: boundary acceptance (ACTIVATION §3), predecessor-claim successor disposition (RELEASE §9), obligation repricing (TICKETS §7), or evaluation planning choice, not coverage status (PROJECT_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASIS_TEMPLATE.md, Coverage And Residuals). missing: absence of a closed result, Not performed (FOUNDATIONS #results). adoption: Product adoption of available profile (PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7), shared method lesson adoption (SPEC_METHOD §6), consumer release-basis adoption (RELEASE §4). Closed/closed: post discussion state (POSTING_GUIDE §7), or ticket/sprint status (TICKETS §4). These locators identify uses rather than supply full definitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":379,
          "quote":"Some labels name different things in different clauses. Each row below\nlocates one sense with the clause that owns it; the label's meaning at an\noccurrence is the sense its owning clause gives, and the occurrence\nqualifies the label or states its predicate where it is used. Equal\nspelling across these rows establishes no equivalence (§1; P1, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F17.%20Labels%20with%20several%20senses",
          "start_line":372
        }
      ],
      "key":"qualified_label_senses",
      "label":"Labels with separately owned senses"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Authored provenance account for rc.2 GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md. Conservation is claimed when every predecessor row is indexed with unchanged bounded-context identity and successor owning locus; per-row owner moves are recorded. Claimed routes: index-contract→§1–2; two method-identity terms→SPEC_METHOD §1, O relation→ODD §1; fourteen calculus rows→§4; seven traversal-profile rows→§15; eight semantic-resolution terms→PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5 except Semantic Import owned at FOUNDATIONS imports; eleven product-definition terms→PRODUCT_DEFINITION §1,3,4,7; seven release terms→RELEASE §2; reference-frame rows→§8 with FOUNDATIONS owners, REFERENCE_FRAME import, EXECUTIVE_BASELINE §11 specialist families and added frame/result/refinement rows; four build-tenancy rows→§9 with Build Tenant at SPEC_METHOD §11, three others at PRODUCT_DEFINITION §8 and work item at TICKETS §4; eight recursive-product terms→FOUNDATIONS Product lineage objects; five assurance rows→ASSURANCE §2 and Unit Test→ASSURANCE §15 with DESIGN_MODULE relation; eight graph-native rows→§14 with ABG relation at ODD §10, F_P/F_H relation at SPEC_METHOD §5 and calculus rows retained; two ambiguity terms→SPEC_METHOD §5; nine world-model terms→WORLD_MODEL §6 with Builder Project specialisation relation. Generalisations remove Index from titles, replace selected complete release with selected complete basis, and move semantic-resolution ownership from SPEC_METHOD to PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5. New §2/§16 routing distinguishes imports, owner relations and members without declared contexts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":424,
          "quote":"Every conserved row is restated in this\nmember's own text; a route is provenance only.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F18.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":410
        }
      ],
      "key":"glossary_conservation_account",
      "label":"Glossary predecessor-conservation account"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Tier-1 member owning identity law: scope, uniqueness, type, stability, readable/opaque forms, hierarchy, engine/domain ownership, authority conservation, overrides/publication, parsing, object/release boundary, minimum questions, examples and implementation consequences. It consumes FOUNDATIONS §2 definitions of Object, Authority and Basis and adds no tier-1 definition. AC-002 applies scoped identity to all calculus identities; calculus-edition identity remains AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §8, and release identity/names/forms remain RELEASE-owned. P3 supplies the identity commitment and P6 authority. rc.2 routes point to IDENTITY_METHOD.md as provenance only, without application depending on that historical cut.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":26,
          "quote":"Tier 1. Owns identity law: scoped identity with its uniqueness, type and\nstability laws; readable and opaque identity; the identity hierarchy;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY",
          "start_line":3
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_member",
      "label":"IDENTITY member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Object identity is always scoped and typed, unique within lawful scope and stable enough for its governed purpose. Its minimum semantic model is authority + kind + value, rather than a globally unique naked string. Additional coordinates can include parent scope, revision, publication version and time.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":39,
          "quote":"Identity is scoped, typed, unique, and stable enough for the governed\npurpose.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F1.%20Position",
          "start_line":30
        },
        {
          "end_line":114,
          "quote":"identity = authority + kind + value",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FScoped%20identity",
          "start_line":109
        }
      ],
      "key":"scoped_object_identity",
      "label":"Scoped object identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F8 identity family table distinguishes foundational consequences from owner choices. Instance premises, not supplied by commitments: (i) projects contain runtime-owned and domain-owned objects of many kinds; (ii) humans/parsers read serialised or readable identities; (iii) release versions belong to releases, not each object. Authored premise map: scope/uniqueness/type←P3+(i); stability←P3,P4b; boundary/sufficiency←P3,P6,P5+(i); readability←P3,P1+(ii); hierarchy←P3+(i),(ii); ownership split←P3,P6+(i); authority conservation←P6,P3,P4b; overrides←P3,P6; parsing←P3,P1+(ii); release distinction←P3,P6+(iii); minimum questions←P3,P6; implementation policy←P3,P1,P0. P1,P3,P6 are independent, P0 recovered, P4b derived from P0/P2, and P5 derived from P0/P6 with P0's open-world premise. Choices belong to object owners for extra coordinates, kinds, form and visible segments; runtime for generation/defaults; domain for grammar, family identities and override publication; issuing authority for decisions/proxy grants; profiles for projection terms; WORLD_MODEL for its Markov refinement; RF-003 for material sufficiency; RELEASE for release forms; this member for the useful hierarchy model, nine grant fields, seven questions and enduring repair policy. Examples and provenance decide nothing beyond their owning rules; consumers cannot enlarge them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":79,
          "quote":"This member is material family F8, identity.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F1.%20Position%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":46
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_foundation_table",
      "label":"F8 foundational derivations and added choices"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Identity kinds illustrated by the source are goal, intent, requirement, requirement_family, acceptance_criterion, scenario, design_decision, published_function, carrier_surface, release, install, run, function_call, continuation and event. The list is illustrative. A family identity is not a concrete requirement identity, and release version is not object identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":137,
          "quote":"Examples of distinct identity kinds\ninclude goal, intent, requirement, requirement_family,\nacceptance_criterion, scenario, design_decision, published_function,\ncarrier_surface, release, install, run, function_call, continuation and\nevent.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FType",
          "start_line":131
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_kinds",
      "label":"Distinct identity kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An object's boundary is a claim about what belongs to it and what its identity does not depend on, carrying a proof obligation. Task-bounded material sufficiency concerns an observed boundary state sufficient for a task. A statistical Markov boundary is the stronger hypothesis that the boundary screens the interior from everything outside.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":164,
          "quote":"An object's boundary, what belongs to the object and what its identity\ndoes not depend on, is a claim with a proof obligation",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FBoundaries%20and%20sufficiency%20claims",
          "start_line":150
        }
      ],
      "key":"object_boundary_claim",
      "label":"Boundary and material-sufficiency claims"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Runtime-owned identities include runs, function calls, jobs, execution frames, continuations and events; the substrate/runtime may define their defaults, opaque identities and local generation rules.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":217,
          "quote":"The substrate or runtime may define default identities for runtime-owned\nobjects, such as runs, function calls, jobs, execution frames,\ncontinuations and events.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F5.%20Authority%20split%2FEngine-owned%20identity",
          "start_line":211
        }
      ],
      "key":"runtime_identity",
      "label":"Engine-owned identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Derived domains own semantic identity grammar for their constitutional objects, including requirements, requirement families, acceptance criteria, scenarios, ADRs/design decisions and published domain assets. They define existing kinds, distinctions, uniqueness and parsing/reading.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":227,
          "quote":"Derived domains own the semantic identity grammar for their own\nconstitutional objects",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F5.%20Authority%20split%2FDomain-owned%20identity",
          "start_line":221
        }
      ],
      "key":"domain_identity",
      "label":"Domain-owned identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Authority is an identity-bearing semantic relation preserving owner, kind, governed subject, scope and governing basis, not a display label, capability string, actor identity or reachable code path. Human authority may act directly or through an explicitly admitted bounded proxy; proxy actor identity remains distinct from human-authority identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":250,
          "quote":"Authority is an identity-bearing semantic relation, not a display label,\ncapability string, actor identity or reachable code path.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F6.%20Authority%20identity%20and%20conservation%20%28STDO-UP-004%29",
          "start_line":231
        }
      ],
      "key":"authority_identity",
      "label":"Authority identity and bounded proxy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Domain override of a bound runtime realisation's default identity format or structure for the domain's own kinds. Defaults are starter conventions and convenience contracts, lawful only while overridable. The runtime's actual published defaults and surfaces are DESIGN_MODULE §12 realisation law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":275,
          "quote":"Default identity formats that a bound runtime realisation publishes are\nstarter conventions and convenience contracts, not universal doctrine;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F7.%20Default%20formats%20and%20overrides%2FDerived%20domain%20overrides",
          "start_line":267
        }
      ],
      "key":"domain_identity_override",
      "label":"Derived-domain identity override"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Release identity is distinct from object identity, publication/revision coordinates and mutable discovery selectors. An immutable release cut is the only exact operative release identifier; release version is a release-process fact. A mutable version-line selector names the highest-ordinal published immutable cut on its line. The source later marks this selector replacement as proposed and pending product-owner acceptance.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":353,
          "quote":"- an immutable release cut is the only exact operative release\n  identifier;\n- the mutable version-line selector only names the highest-ordinal\n  published immutable cut on its line (FOUNDATIONS §2);",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F9.%20Release%20version%20boundary",
          "start_line":343
        }
      ],
      "key":"release_identity_boundary",
      "label":"Release/object identity boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "rc.2 IDENTITY_METHOD.md conservation account, not an acceptance act. Generalisations: graph_function→published_function, asset_surface→carrier_surface, graph_call→function_call; graph calls→function calls; frames→execution frames, distinct from FOUNDATIONS frames; graph-native F_H projection→a profile's optional projection under its own term; bound ABG realisations→bound runtime realisations; Id→identity. Predecessor must becomes indicative while should, may, at minimum, at most, optional, useful and suitable retain force. Position, scope, uniqueness, type, stability, readability, hierarchy, ownership split, UP-004, overrides/publication, parsing, minimum questions, examples and implementation consequences are claimed conserved at corresponding successor clauses. Heading-only core-law, authority-split, default-formats-and-overrides and examples are recorded not applicable. Runtime-default publication permission moves to DESIGN_MODULE §12; overridability law remains here. Release selector wording is proposed supersession pending owner acceptance; release naming forms belong to RELEASE §1. The account claims six authority prohibitions and seven decision transitions, but does not enumerate all foundational prohibitions here.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":438,
          "quote":"the\npredecessor's \"must\" is written in the indicative and its \"should\",\n\"may\", \"at minimum\", \"at most\", \"optional\", \"useful\" and \"suitable\" keep\ntheir force.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F13.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":428
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_conservation_account",
      "label":"Identity predecessor-conservation account"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
